n../docs/proposal.md:310:**One pool of job slots, drained from the end.** At most `max_pods` jobs (default 3) run at once, shared by every loop. When a slot frees, the controller fills it in this order: reviewers, then workers, then triage, then scanners, oldest first within each. Finishing work in flight beats starting new work, so a busy day grows the scanner backlog, not the pile of half-done PRs. The merger and the Reaper are controller code and take no slot. Slots are granted when a loop polls and nothing is queued: a loop that found work waiting within the last two of its intervals has that demand reserved ahead of lower classes, so a freed slot would otherwise sit idle until that loop's next poll. To avoid that, a released slot (or a raised `max_pods`) wakes the loops with live demand for one tick, after a short debounce (default 2s) so a burst of releases makes one wake; those loops offer as usual, so class order and reservations still decide, and a paused controller ignores the wake. The merger, prwatch and scanners are never woken this way. A scanner run that gets no slot is skipped, not queued. The pool also starts no agent job for an exhausted account or model family until its reset: a job that stops at a subscription usage limit records the limit (in the controller database, so a restart keeps it), and the pool answers `limited` to any candidate whose role runs on that account or family (a whole-account session or weekly limit blocks every role; an Opus or Sonnet limit blocks only roles on that family, and a role with no model, which runs the CLI default, is blocked by any limit). A limited candidate takes no slot and reserves no demand, so lower classes are not held back. The block ends at the reset time the CLI reported; when it reported none the first pause is 30 minutes and each further stop with no reset doubles it up to 4 hours, starting over once a job succeeds; a reset time further away than 8 days is clamped. Running jobs are never killed for a limit. Deterministic scanners and the merger use no model and are unaffected. A daily dollar cap works the same way at the same gate: the pool answers `budget` to a candidate whose role cap or the repo cap is reached, and that candidate takes no slot and reserves no demand. A job that already holds a slot keeps it and finishes.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1a4c5516-9c06-4575-8780-911f72b7bbd6","uuid":"b0b3e392-3679-4b15-9f6c-ba29e3ea4b53","timestamp":"2026-11-25T13:41:24.211Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"\t\t}\n\t\treturn p.Review.CommitSHA, true, nil\n\t}\n\treturn \"\", false, nil\n}\n\n// Deps are the collaborators of a Closer. Prepare needs Roles, NewRedactor,\n// Changed, BaseFile and StagingDir; the rest are for Closer.\ntype Deps struct {\n\tEffects   Effects\n\tComments  Comments\n\tCommitter Committer\n\tStore     Store\n\tRoles     RoleSet\n\t// NewRedactor builds the redactor for a job, with the executor's secret\n\t// values (rebuilt, never persisted). Resume does not call it: it uses\n\t// only already-redacted staged bytes.\n\tNewRedactor func(Job) (Redactor, error)\n\t// Changed lists paths under a pathspec changed since a base commit\n\t// (gitx.Git.ChangedSince).\n\tChanged func(ctx context.Context, worktree, base string, paths ...string) ([]gitx.PathChange, error)\n\t// BaseFile reads one file at a commit (gitx.Git.FileAt): ok is false when\n\t// the path does not exist there. Prepare uses it to compare plan.md with\n\t// its base.\n\tBaseFile func(ctx context.Context, worktree, rev, path string, maxBytes int64) (data []byte, ok bool, err error)\n\t// PR reads a pull request (github.Reader.GetPR). Optional: a review's\n\t// close-out uses it to notice a PR merged or closed mid-review. nil skips\n\t// the check.\n\tPR         func(ctx context.Context, n int) (github.PullRequest, error)\n\tRepo       github.Repo\n\tStagingDir string\n\tLog        *slog.Logger\n\tNow        func() time.Time\n}\n\nfunc (d Deps) log() *